00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.260
Former Federal Reserve Chair Alan Greenspan's approach to managing market expectations during crises

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.880
continues to shape discussions about central bank communication.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:40.800
Outlets across the spectrum note his skill at steering policy through the 1987 crash and later

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.680
expansions. CNBC frames his measured public statements as a deliberate tool that

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.280
preserved flexibility for policy makers across multiple administrations.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:58.240
The Guardian emphasizes his influence stretching from the Reagan era through George W. Bush,

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.580
setting lasting precedence for how the institution speaks to markets.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.580
Treasury officials and former colleagues argue his steady presence demonstrated the value of

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:15.240
experienced non-partisan leadership at the Fed. Civil liberties and transparency advocates

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.040
counter that his reluctance to speak plainly reduced accountability over decisions affecting wages

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.480
and savings. Even supporters can see that later events tested the limits of the hands-off regulatory

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.840
stance he favored. What evidence shows whether his statements moved markets more than formal announcements?

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.320
Shifting now to diplomacy in the Middle East, Israel and Lebanon are scheduled for a new round

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.460
of US hosted talks aimed at addressing security and reconstruction after years of conflict.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.860
Israeli officials view the discussions as a chance to secure lasting guarantees,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.480
while Lebanese representatives see them as a way to ease military pressure and begin

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:04.040
repairing infrastructure. US diplomats emphasize procedural continuity with past agreements that

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:10.180
produce temporary ceasefires. Iranian statements highlight the need for parallel economic measures

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:16.940
to sustain any deal. Both sides accept that external mediation has previously paused hostilities,

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.540
yet they differ on whether the current round can establish durable borders.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:27.720
The question worth considering is what concrete security arrangements are actually on the table

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:35.220
and which outside actors retain veto power. Meanwhile, the Senate passed a non-binding resolution

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.080
addressing prolonged military engagement without fresh legislative approval.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:46.200
Supporters in both parties emphasize restoring checks on executive decisions that could expand

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:52.640
into broader conflict. Critics within the administration view the step as an unnecessary constraint

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.880
that weakens leverage during active talks. Treasury and defense officials maintain that sustained

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:05.240
pressure through sanctions and military posture has brought Iran to the table. Congressional

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:11.060
backers of the resolution counter that unchecked executive latitude has historically led to mission

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:17.080
creep. Both sides agree the vote carries no binding legal force, but they differ on whether it

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:23.960
strengthens or complicates diplomatic efforts. How have past Senate votes on war powers affected

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:30.920
actual military planning? Next, Venezuela faces recovery after a major earthquake that authorities

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:37.200
have described as a national emergency. neighboring governments and aid organizations frame their

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:44.380
offers of help as standard humanitarian practice. Some regional analysts point to political isolation

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:49.440
that has limited Venezuela's access to routine disaster cooperation channels.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.680
The strongest argument for swift international aid rests on the narrow time windows for live rescues

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:02.860
after collapse structures. Officials wary of external involvement stress that aid deliveries must

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:09.540
